==Analysis==
While Andrews benefitted from record-high turnout, primarily from Democrats, to flip the state, she was the only Democrat to win any of the [[2026 South Carolina elections|statewide elections that year]]. Andrews won seven counties that [[Alan Wilson (politician)|Alan Wilson]] won in the [[2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election|concurrent gubernatorial election]]. Overall, Andrews won a total of 25 counties, several of which voted for [[Donald Trump]] by large margins in [[2024 United States presidential election in South Carolina|2024]], including [[Clarendon County, South Carolina|Clarendon County]].<ref>{{cite news |title=A look at how SC voters' support shifted to flip the US Senate seat |url=https://scdailygazette.com/2026/11/06/a-look-at-how-sc-voters-support-shifted-to-flip-the-us-senate-seat/ |access-date=November 10, 2026 |publisher=South Carolina Daily Gazette |date=November 6, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Rural Voters Helped Democrats Win South Carolina's U.S. Senate Seat |url=https://dailyyonder.com/rural-voters-helped-democrats-win-south-carolinas-us-senate-seat/2026/11/04/ |access-date=November 10, 2026 |publisher=Daily Yonder |date=November 4, 2026}}</ref> Extremely high turnout from Black voters in the state, nearly at the level of a presidential election, was also seen as instrumental in Andrews' win.<ref>{{cite news |title=South Carolina's Black vote turned out at presidential levels. Can Democrats stay engaged for 2028? |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/sc-black-turnout-midterm-2028-primary/article_fe708b25-c6bc-4b32-bb07-76d4f448fdeb.html |access-date=November 10, 2026 |publisher=Post and Courier |date=November 8, 2026}}</ref>

